# Programs for people and machines

A program has at least two audiences. A computer needs instructions in a precise
order and form. A person needs to understand the problem, the choices that shaped
the solution, and the way its parts relate. Those needs overlap, but they are not
the same.

Conventional source files usually give the computer's order authority. We may add
comments, separate documentation, diagrams, and examples, but the file remains the
spine around which the explanation must fit. This works well for many programs.
For a system whose reasoning matters as much as its syntax, however, the most
helpful explanation may need a different order.

Literate programming turns that relationship around. The author writes an
explanation and places pieces of the program where they illuminate the ideas being
discussed. Named [fragments](term:fragment) connect those pieces. A tool later arranges them into
the source files expected by compilers and other language tools.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Questions[Questions a reader asks] --> Explanation[Explanatory order]
    Explanation --> Fragments[Named program fragments]
    Build[Order required by tools] --> Source[Generated source files]
    Fragments --> Source
    Explanation --> Book[Readable book]
```

The upper path in this diagram is organized by understanding: what problem are we
solving, what constraints matter, and why does one idea lead to the next? The lower
path represents the order needed to build and run the program. Named fragments are
the bridge between them.

## Explanation becomes the source

In a literate program, prose is not a commentary added after the implementation.
It is part of the authoritative work. The code fragments shown among that prose
are authoritative too. The generated source file is important—it is what ordinary
language tools consume—but it is a result rather than the primary place where the
author develops the program.

Two traditional words describe the outputs:

- **Tangling** follows fragment references to produce program source.
- **Weaving** presents prose, diagrams, and fragments as a document for readers.

The names come from textiles: separate threads become a useful whole. One process
arranges code for the machine; the other arranges the same work for people.

```NOTE
Donald Knuth's WEB system popularized the textile words *tangle* and *weave* in
literate programming. The metaphor has lasted rather better than many computing
terms from the same period.
```

This does not free the author from structure. It creates a different responsibility.
The explanation must introduce ideas in a helpful order, and fragment names must
make their relationships intelligible. Poor prose and arbitrary fragments can turn
a literate program into source code hidden inside a more complicated container.
The method helps only when the book is maintained with the same care as the code.

## Why named fragments matter

An anonymous code sample says, “here are some statements.” A named fragment says,
“this is the part of the program that carries out a particular idea.” Its name can
participate in the prose and in a larger fragment:

```text
\<<process a literate project>>=
    \<<capture the project snapshot>>
    \<<build and validate the fragment graph>>
    \<<plan every generated output>>
    \<<commit the output transaction>>
```

In the actual notation these phrases are visibly marked as fragment references.
Read as prose, they form an outline of the mechanism. Read by Literate, they name
the pieces from which the implementation is assembled.

This dual role is central. Fragment names are not labels attached only for the
build system. They are part of the vocabulary with which the book explains the
program.

## What the method promises

A well-written literate program can keep several forms of knowledge together:

- the purpose of a component;
- the assumptions on which it relies;
- alternatives that were rejected;
- diagrams of relationships and lifecycles;
- formulas or measurements that justify a limit;
- the implementation of each decision;
- examples and tests that make the contract concrete.

Because the explanation and implementation are edited together, a design decision
need not live in a distant document that gradually stops matching the code. The
book can also introduce a concept before its implementation location would permit
in an ordinary source file.

Literate itself is the running example in this book. Its fragments describe a
language, a deterministic generator, an editor environment, and a publication
system. The program is large enough to show where literate programming helps and
where it creates new difficulties.

## What the method costs

The generated file is no longer the whole source of truth, so contributors need
tools that understand fragments. Errors can occur in the fragment graph as well as
in the generated programming language. A reader may need to follow a reference to
another chapter. Bootstrapping requires care when the generator is itself generated.

There is also a subtler cost: completeness can be mistaken for explanation. A book
may contain every line of code and still make the program harder to understand.
Assembly manifests, imports, registrations, and protocol plumbing are necessary,
but they do not all deserve equal weight in the main narrative.

This book therefore separates its roles. The main chapters concentrate on method,
architecture, algorithms, and important boundaries. The appendices retain the
mechanical maps and detailed references needed to assemble and audit the complete
program.

## Where literate programming fits

The method is especially valuable when reasoning must survive for a long time:
complex algorithms, unusual safety boundaries, scientific software, systems with
several interacting representations, or projects meant to teach as well as run.
It can also help whenever the best order for introducing ideas differs strongly
from the best order for a compiler.

It is not automatically the right choice for every task. A tiny disposable script
may need no additional structure. Mechanically generated code may be better
explained by its generator. A team unwilling to maintain the prose will gain
little from making prose authoritative.

The next chapter uses a small complete example. It begins with an everyday task,
divides that task into named ideas, and follows those ideas into an executable
source file and a readable document.
